WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.750
Your phone can run real Python,

2
00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.970
the same language used by

3
00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.830
scientists, game makers and banks.

4
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.240
Today you will write your first program.

5
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.120
It will be one line long.

6
00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:16.750
Today: your first Python program.

7
00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.630
The instruction print shows

8
00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:20.670
something on the screen.

9
00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:23.430
Put text inside quotes and

10
00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.650
brackets, exactly like this.

11
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.520
Press Run, and the computer does

12
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:29.880
exactly what you wrote.

13
00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:31.160
That’s a program!

14
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.340
A program can have many lines.

15
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.740
Python runs them one at a

16
00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.480
time, from the top down.

17
00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.210
Change the order of the lines, and the

18
00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:42.870
order on the screen changes too.

19
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:45.890
Python is also a calculator.

20
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.940
Plus and minus are as you know them.

21
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.050
A star means times, and a

22
00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.150
slash means divide.

23
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.060
Two stars mean to the power of: two, star

24
00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.460
star, ten is one thousand and twenty-four.

25
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:03.790
Careful with quotes.

26
00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:06.770
With quotes, Python shows the

27
00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.490
text exactly as it is.

28
00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.790
Without quotes, it works the sum out.

29
00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.970
So the first line shows seven plus

30
00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.530
five, and the second shows twelve.

31
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:19.330
Quick check!

32
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:24.120
The line is print, two plus three

33
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.220
times four, with no quotes.

34
00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:28.930
What does it show?

35
00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:30.740
Fourteen.

36
00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.020
Python follows the same order as maths:

37
00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.080
times and divide before plus and minus.

38
00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.840
If you want plus first, use

39
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.160
brackets: two plus three, in

40
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.600
brackets, times four is twenty.

41
00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:48.530
With just print and maths, you can

42
00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.290
already do useful things.

43
00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:52.490
Work out how many minutes

44
00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:53.570
are in a school year.

45
00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:55.730
Print a poster for a school event.

46
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.340
Check your maths homework.

47
00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.510
Find out how many seconds old you are.

48
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.740
Sometimes Python stops with

49
00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:04.920
a red error message.

50
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.880
Don’t worry: everyone gets them.

51
00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:11.130
The last line says what’s wrong, and

52
00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.090
it tells you the line number.

53
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.300
A missing quote or bracket

54
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.560
is the most common.

55
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.600
In the app, open Step through a program.

56
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.940
Press Run and record, then Next, and

57
00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.300
watch Python run one line at a time.

58
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.000
Then change the code to your own

59
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.340
prints and sums, and run it again.

60
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:33.310
Your turn.

61
00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.370
What does print, with ten

62
00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.570
times two in quotes, show?

63
00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.260
Ten times two, just as written.

64
00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.660
Remember: print shows

65
00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:45.720
things on the screen.

66
00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:49.550
Python runs your lines from top to bottom.

67
00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:51.430
Text goes in quotes.

68
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.780
And times comes before plus,

69
00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:56.540
unless you use brackets.

70
00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.400
Try it yourself: work out how many

71
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.520
hours you spend at school in a year,

72
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.180
with one line of Python.

73
00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.740
Write your guess on paper first.

74
00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.700
Then run it, and see how close you were.
